12
00:51:00,000 --> 00:51:06,000
Alice's Adventures in Wonderland (also known as  Alice in Wonderland) is an 1865 English
children's novel by Lewis Carroll, a mathematics don at the University of Oxford.

13
00:55:15,000 --> 00:55:21,000
It details the story of a girl named Alice who falls through a rabbit
hole into a fantasy world of anthropomorphic creatures.

14
00:59:30,000 --> 00:59:36,000
It is seen as an example of the literary nonsense genre.

15
01:03:45,000 --> 01:03:51,000
The artist John Tenniel provided 42 wood-engraved illustrations for the book.

16
01:08:00,000 --> 01:08:06,000
The novel received positive reviews upon release and is now one of the best-known works of Victorian literature; its narrative,
structure, characters and imagery have had a wide influence on popular culture and literature, especially in the fantasy genre.

17
01:12:15,000 --> 01:12:21,000
It is credited as helping end an era of didacticism in children's literature,
inaugurating an era in which writing for children aimed to "delight or entertain".

18
01:16:30,000 --> 01:16:36,000
The tale plays with logic, giving the story lasting
popularity with adults as well as with children.

19
01:20:45,000 --> 01:20:51,000
The titular character Alice shares her name with Alice Liddell, a girl Carroll knew;
scholars disagree about the extent to which the character was based upon her.

20
01:25:00,000 --> 01:25:06,000
The novel has never been out of print and has
been translated into 174 languages.

21
01:29:15,000 --> 01:29:21,000
Its legacy includes adaptations to screen, radio, visual art, ballet, opera, and
musical theatre, as well as theme parks, board games and video games.

22
01:33:30,000 --> 01:33:36,000
Carroll published a sequel in 1871 entitled Through the Looking-Glass and a
shortened version for young children, The Nursery "Alice", in 1890.

23
01:37:45,000 --> 01:37:51,000
The tone is emotional.
